---
// /guides/how-to-secure-ai-agents
//
// Answer-engine landing for the highest-volume "how do I secure my
// AI agent" query. Structured for extraction: TL;DR → 6-step HowTo →
// per-step detail → FAQ → CTA. Ships HowTo + FAQPage + BreadcrumbList
// JSON-LD so ChatGPT SGE, Google AI Overviews, and Perplexity can
// surface it as a rich result.
//
// Facts are anchored to arXiv:2603.12621 (the AEGIS paper) and OWASP
// LLM Top-10 (2025 revision) so the extractable claims are
// externally verifiable.

import Layout from '@/layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '@/components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '@/components/Footer.astro';

const canonicalUrl = 'https://aegistraces.com/guides/how-to-secure-ai-agents';

const STEPS = [
  {
    title: 'Enumerate the tool-call surface',
    duration: '15 min',
    body: 'List every tool your agent can call — database, shell, HTTP, email send, file write, code execution. Group into read-only, write, and side-effectful. Read-only tools default to allow; write and side-effectful tools go through classification.',
    concrete: 'For a LangChain agent: iterate agent.tools and label each. For Anthropic tool_use: enumerate tools[] in the API call. For OpenAI: iterate tools[] in the response. This step is required — 86% of harm in agent deployments happens at the tool-call boundary, not in the prompt.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Instrument the framework with a tool-call gateway',
    duration: '10 min',
    body: 'Add a runtime layer between the LLM\'s tool-call decision and the tool\'s execution. In the AEGIS SDK this is a two-line change: import agentguard; agentguard.auto(). The SDK monkey-patches all detected framework SDKs at import time; existing agent code is unchanged.',
    concrete: 'Supported frameworks: Anthropic, OpenAI, LangChain, CrewAI, Gemini, Bedrock, Mistral, LlamaIndex, smolagents (Python); Anthropic, OpenAI, LangChain, Vercel AI SDK (JS/TS); Go. See https://github.com/Justin0504/Aegis for the full list.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Configure a three-layer classification cascade',
    duration: '30 min',
    body: 'A single detector is either too brittle (regex misses 98%+ of real attacks in our benchmark) or too expensive (LLM judges cost $0.36–$5.32 per run at 1–2 s latency). The Pareto-optimal defence is a cost-aware cascade: deterministic rules for known-bad patterns (L1), a supervised classifier over structural features (L2), and an LLM judge for the small ambiguous residual (L3).',
    concrete: 'AEGIS ships this cascade out of the box: L1 = 22 patterns × 7 categories, L2 = XGBoost over 15 features, L3 = Claude Haiku. On the 5,525-record ToolGuard-Bench (arXiv:2603.12621), the cascade achieves 99.9% block rate at 1.06 ms P50 latency and $0.05 total run cost.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Add a human-in-the-loop approval queue for high-risk calls',
    duration: '20 min',
    body: 'Not every risky call should be auto-blocked — some legitimate calls look suspicious (e.g. an admin script deleting old records). Route calls above a risk threshold to a pending queue where a reviewer sees the full argument JSON, risk signals, and can approve or reject in one polling cycle.',
    concrete: 'The AEGIS Compliance Cockpit ships this queue with SDK-level suspension: the agent pauses execution while the reviewer decides, then resumes on approval within one polling cycle (typically 2 s). Reviewers can grant one-shot approval or persist the decision as a new policy rule.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Log every decision to a tamper-evident audit chain',
    duration: '10 min',
    body: 'Plain database logging fails covered-entity review (HIPAA §164.312(b)) and EU AI Act Article 12 compliance because a database row is trivially editable by anyone with DB access. Use a cryptographic hash chain (SHA-256) with per-agent Ed25519 signatures so the audit log is third-party verifiable — the standard RFC 6962 uses for SSL certificate transparency.',
    concrete: 'AEGIS ships a 245-line Node stdlib verifier that runs air-gapped; auditors can validate Merkle inclusion + consistency proofs without trusting AEGIS. See https://github.com/Justin0504/Aegis/tree/main/tools/verify-log.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Wire alerts on repeated blocks + PII exposure to Slack / PagerDuty',
    duration: '15 min',
    body: 'A compromised agent typically triggers many blocks in a short window before the operator notices. Alerting on ≥3 blocks per agent per hour catches compromise early; alerting on any PII-in-args event catches the accidental leak scenarios.',
    concrete: 'AEGIS ships native destinations for Slack (Incoming Webhooks) and PagerDuty (Events API v2). Configuration is in the Compliance Cockpit → Settings → Alert Rules. Both destinations are open-source; no vendor lock-in.',
  },
];

const FAQ = [
  {
    q: 'What is the fastest way to secure an AI agent?',
    a: 'Add a tool-call gateway with a two-line SDK change (import agentguard; agentguard.auto()). Deploy the three-layer cascade (rules → XGBoost → LLM judge) which reaches 99.9% block rate on ToolGuard-Bench at 1.06 ms P50 latency and $0.05 total cost. The Compliance Cockpit provides the human-review UI and tamper-evident audit chain out of the box.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need to change my agent code?',
    a: 'No. The AEGIS SDK monkey-patches supported frameworks at import time via agentguard.auto(). Existing agent code — LangChain graphs, CrewAI flows, Anthropic tool_use blocks, OpenAI function calls — runs unchanged. Only the two-line initialiser is added.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which frameworks are supported?',
    a: 'Fourteen frameworks: Anthropic, OpenAI, LangChain, CrewAI, Gemini, Bedrock, Mistral, LlamaIndex, smolagents (Python); Anthropic SDK, OpenAI SDK, LangChain, Vercel AI SDK (JavaScript / TypeScript); Go. New framework support ships every ~2 weeks; the current matrix is at github.com/Justin0504/Aegis.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How does this compare to Lakera Guard or NeMo Guardrails?',
    a: 'AEGIS is a runtime firewall on the tool-call execution path with a cryptographic audit chain. Lakera Guard is a managed content-safety API without a self-hostable engine. NeMo Guardrails uses the Colang DSL for conversational flow control (topical / input / output / retrieval / execution rails). Head-to-head deltas at /vs/lakera-guard and /vs/nemo-guardrails.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I self-host without any egress?',
    a: 'Yes. AEGIS runs as a single binary plus optional Postgres. The audit chain uses Ed25519 signatures and SHA-256 hash-chaining verifiable with a 245-line Node stdlib script — no calls to third parties are required for enforcement or verification. This is the mode used by regulated deployments in fintech, healthcare, and government.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What compliance frameworks does this satisfy?',
    a: 'SOC 2 Type II (Enterprise tier ships the report), PCI-DSS v4.0 Req 8 + 10, HIPAA §164.312 (all 5 implementation specs mapped), EU AI Act Articles 12–15 (signed evidence pack), GDPR Article 22 (automated decision-making transparency).',
  },
  {
    q: 'How is a runtime firewall different from an LLM guardrail?',
    a: 'A guardrail (NeMo, Guardrails AI, Llama Guard) filters or repairs text — input from the user, output from the model. A runtime firewall (AEGIS) filters actions — tool calls that would otherwise cause side effects. Both are useful; most production stacks use both, because they defend at different points in the pipeline.',
  },
];

const howToLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'HowTo',
  '@id': `${canonicalUrl}#howto`,
  name: 'How to Secure an AI Agent',
  description: 'Six practical steps to deploy runtime safety on an LLM agent — from enumerating the tool-call surface to alerting on repeated blocks.',
  totalTime: 'PT100M',
  step: STEPS.map((s, i) => ({
    '@type': 'HowToStep',
    position: i + 1,
    name: s.title,
    text: `${s.body} ${s.concrete}`,
  })),
};

const faqLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  '@id': `${canonicalUrl}#faq`,
  mainEntity: FAQ.map(({ q, a }) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: a },
  })),
  speakable: {
    '@type': 'SpeakableSpecification',
    cssSelector: ['.g-tldr p', '.step-body', '.faq-item p'],
  },
};
---

<Layout
  title="How to Secure an AI Agent — 6 Steps (2026 Guide)"
  description="Six practical steps to deploy runtime safety on an LLM agent: enumerate tool-call surface, instrument with a gateway, configure a three-layer cascade (99.9% block at 1.06 ms), route pending calls to a reviewer, log to a tamper-evident audit chain, alert on repeated blocks. Facts anchored to arXiv:2603.12621."
  canonical={canonicalUrl}
  ogImage="/og-image.png"
>
  <Header />
  <main>
    <section class="g-hero">
      <div class="container container-narrow">
        <p class="eyebrow">Guide · updated 2026-09</p>
        <h1>How to secure an AI agent</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Six steps, roughly 100 minutes to complete, one file to
          change. If you have five minutes: install the AEGIS SDK,
          run <code>agentguard.auto()</code>, and every tool call
          your agent makes is now mediated by a three-layer cascade
          that blocks 99.9% of malicious calls at 1.06 ms P50 latency
          (see <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.12621">arXiv:2603.12621</a>).
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="g-tldr">
      <div class="container container-narrow">
        <h2>TL;DR</h2>
        <p>
          Add a tool-call gateway between the LLM's decision to call
          a tool and the tool's execution. Configure a three-layer
          cascade (deterministic rules → supervised classifier → LLM
          judge) so most calls resolve in &lt; 1 ms and only the
          ambiguous residual reaches the expensive judge. Route
          high-risk calls to a human reviewer. Log every decision to
          a cryptographic hash chain so auditors can verify the log
          was not modified after the fact.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="g-steps">
      <div class="container container-narrow">
        <h2>The six steps</h2>
        <ol class="step-list">
          {STEPS.map((s, i) => (
            <li class="step">
              <div class="step-head">
                <span class="step-num">{i + 1}</span>
                <div>
                  <h3>{s.title}</h3>
                  <p class="step-meta">Estimated: {s.duration}</p>
                </div>
              </div>
              <p class="step-body">{s.body}</p>
              <p class="step-concrete"><strong>Concretely:</strong> {s.concrete}</p>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ol>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="g-faq">
      <div class="container container-narrow">
        <h2>Frequently asked</h2>
        {FAQ.map(({ q, a }) => (
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>{q}</summary>
            <p>{a}</p>
          </details>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="g-cta">
      <div class="container container-narrow">
        <h2>Deploy AEGIS</h2>
        <p>MIT-licensed engine. Two-line SDK integration. Free forever.</p>
        <div class="ctas">
          <a class="btn" href="/download">Download AEGIS</a>
          <a class="btn-secondary" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.12621">Read the paper</a>
          <a class="btn-secondary" href="https://github.com/Justin0504/Aegis">GitHub source</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
  <Footer />

  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(howToLd)}></script>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqLd)}></script>
</Layout>

<style>
  main { background: hsl(42 45% 92%); }
  .g-hero { padding: 64px 0 32px; }
  .eyebrow { text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.14em; font-size: 12px; color: hsl(20 15% 40%); margin: 0 0 12px; }
  h1 { font-family: 'Instrument Serif', Georgia, serif; font-size: 46px; font-weight: 400; margin: 0 0 16px; line-height: 1.1; }
  .lede { font-size: 17px; line-height: 1.6; color: hsl(20 10% 30%); max-width: 720px; }
  .lede code { background: hsl(42 30% 88%); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px; font-size: 14.5px; }

  .g-tldr { padding: 24px 0; }
  .g-tldr h2 { font-size: 20px; margin: 0 0 8px; }
  .g-tldr p { padding: 14px 16px; background: hsl(42 30% 96%); border: 1px solid hsl(42 20% 82%); border-radius: 8px; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6; margin: 0; }

  .g-steps { padding: 40px 0 16px; }
  .g-steps h2 { font-family: 'Instrument Serif', Georgia, serif; font-weight: 400; font-size: 30px; margin: 0 0 24px; }
  .step-list { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; display: grid; gap: 16px; counter-reset: step; }
  .step { background: white; border: 1px solid hsl(42 20% 82%); border-radius: 8px; padding: 20px; }
  .step-head { display: flex; gap: 14px; align-items: flex-start; margin: 0 0 10px; }
  .step-num { flex: 0 0 34px; height: 34px; border-radius: 50%; background: hsl(20 45% 30%); color: white; display: grid; place-items: center; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; }
  .step-head h3 { margin: 0 0 2px; font-size: 17px; font-weight: 600; color: hsl(20 30% 20%); }
  .step-meta { margin: 0; font-size: 11px; color: hsl(20 15% 50%); text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.05em; }
  .step-body { margin: 0 0 10px; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.6; color: hsl(20 10% 25%); }
  .step-concrete { margin: 0; padding: 10px 12px; background: hsl(42 30% 96%); border-left: 3px solid hsl(20 45% 40%); border-radius: 4px; font-size: 13.5px; line-height: 1.55; color: hsl(20 10% 30%); }
  .step-concrete strong { color: hsl(20 40% 25%); }

  .g-faq { padding: 48px 0 16px; }
  .g-faq h2 { font-family: 'Instrument Serif', Georgia, serif; font-weight: 400; font-size: 30px; margin: 0 0 16px; }
  .faq-item { border-bottom: 1px solid hsl(42 20% 82%); padding: 12px 0; }
  .faq-item summary { cursor: pointer; font-size: 15px; font-weight: 500; color: hsl(20 30% 25%); list-style: none; padding: 4px 0; }
  .faq-item summary::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }
  .faq-item summary::before { content: '+ '; color: hsl(20 40% 40%); font-weight: 600; margin-right: 6px; }
  .faq-item[open] summary::before { content: '– '; }
  .faq-item p { margin: 8px 0 0; padding: 0 0 6px 16px; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.6; color: hsl(20 10% 30%); }

  .g-cta { padding: 40px 0 80px; text-align: center; }
  .g-cta h2 { font-family: 'Instrument Serif', Georgia, serif; font-weight: 400; font-size: 28px; margin: 0 0 8px; }
  .g-cta p { color: hsl(20 10% 40%); font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 20px; }
  .ctas { display: flex; gap: 10px; justify-content: center; flex-wrap: wrap; }
  .btn, .btn-secondary { display: inline-block; padding: 10px 18px; border-radius: 6px; text-decoration: none; font-size: 14px; font-weight: 500; }
  .btn { background: hsl(20 45% 20%); color: white; }
  .btn-secondary { background: transparent; color: hsl(20 30% 25%); border: 1px solid hsl(42 20% 70%); }
</style>
